Author:     Kyle Gruel <kylegruel@gmail.com>
AuthorDate: Tue Aug 11 15:57:56 2026 -0500
Commit:     Kyle Gruel <kylegruel@gmail.com>
CommitDate: Tue Aug 11 15:57:56 2026 -0500

    feat(engine): canonical_audit — judge the derived index against the log
    
    A JSONL-canonical store has two artifacts and, until now, one verifier: every
    surface walked the derived sqlite index alone, so a row the canonical log
    never carried still rendered "chain intact" at rc=0 — a false attestation of
    exactly the lie-class the chain exists to prevent.
    
    canonical_audit is the reader that judges the two against each other, at two
    depths: audit_agreement (offset parity, count parity, last-line agreement —
    O(1)-ish, cheap enough to gate every read verb) and audit_deep (every log
    line compared field-for-field and in order against the index, then the tick
    chain re-derived from CANONICAL content rather than from the index it is
    judging).
    
    Pure reader by contract: it never constructs a JsonlStore. That constructor
    repairs — catch-up, torn-line truncate, rebuild — and repair destroys the
    evidence verification inspects; open-time recovery and verification are
    opposite contracts and must not share a code path. The log is read with plain
    file IO through the codec, the index read-only, and no marker is written.
    
    The marker keys and row_matches move here as their one spelling; JsonlStore
    imports the keys and delegates _row_matches, so reconciliation and audit
    cannot drift into two definitions of "the same row".
    
    design/store/verify-canonical-agreement (L1 + --deep layers)
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

