    fix(engine,loops): judge the consumed prefix; lag is not tampering
    
    `_check_last_line` judged the log's FINAL line rather than the last line
    the index consumed. After a crash between the log's fsync and the index
    commit — a state jsonl_store documents as normal — the unindexed tail has
    no index row by construction, so the audit printed "the index was edited
    out of band" about an index nobody touched, and reported the same offset
    divergence twice. A torn tail read as "incomplete or unreadable" for
    bytes the next open exists to truncate. The bound is now the stamped
    offset: the only prefix the index ever claimed to account for.
    
    Classification follows. `Check.lag` marks a divergence as the writer's own
    recovery window (offset < size, and in --deep the content failures on
    lines beyond it); `AgreementReport.lag_only` is true when every divergence
    is one. rc=1 and the chain-walk suppression are unchanged (ratified in
    design:store/verify-canonical-agreement — a behind index still cannot
    serve what the log holds), but the framing splits: INDEX BEHIND THE LOG
    with the repair named ("loops read <vertex>") instead of CANONICAL
    DISAGREEMENT, on verify and on the stats/ticks gate alike. The route out
    existed in-product all along; the refusal just never said so.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

